---
/**
 * Logo — the three logo families from Figma section 471:112, behind one prop API.
 *
 *   kind="mark"      24 square product marks   (12 products × colour/mono)
 *   kind="wordmark"  5 wordmark lockups        (iron: default/ondark/mono · suite: default/ondark)
 *   kind="element"   13 illustrative elements
 *   kind="lockup"    2 product lockups         (basic = horizontal · stack = vertical)
 *
 * Unlike the rest of the library this component does ship a reference to real
 * files — a logo *is* its artwork, there is no token that can stand in for it.
 * `basePath` points at wherever `docs/assets/` was copied to, same escape hatch
 * as Footer.astro's *ImgSrc props.
 *
 * ── Why this component declares no `figma-size` band ──────────────────────
 *
 * Its size ladder is fixed, and it already has a better gate than a rendered
 * band: `check:logo-grid` DERIVES the ladder from the SVGs themselves rather
 * than comparing it to numbers written down anywhere. A band here would restate
 * what that already proves, against a box that injected padding cannot move.
 */

type Product = 'pdf' | 'wd' | 'xl' | 'ppt' | 'qr' | 'ocr' | 'bc' | 'prt' | 'zip' | 'ws' | 'suite' | 'iron';
type ElementName =
  | 'pdf' | 'xl' | 'word' | 'ocr' | 'qr' | 'barcode' | 'zip'
  | 'print' | 'drawing' | 'webscraper' | 'ppt' | 'securedoc' | 'freetools';

interface Props {
  kind?: 'mark' | 'wordmark' | 'element' | 'lockup';
  /** Required for kind="mark" (Product) and kind="element" (ElementName). */
  product?: Product | ElementName;
  /** kind="wordmark" only. */
  brand?: 'iron' | 'suite';
  /** Figma's variant axis, scoped to the kind: wordmark takes
   *  default/ondark/mono/onhero (`mono` and `onhero` are iron-only — suite has
   *  neither in Figma); lockup takes basic/stack.
   *
   *  `onhero` is the colour mark beside an ALL-WHITE wordmark, added 2026-08-21
   *  (Figma `Main logo` variant `Onhero`). It exists because `ondark` is not
   *  enough over PHOTOGRAPHY: it keeps SOFTWARE on `brand/primary`, which
   *  measures 1.21:1 against a warm image. That is not fixable by treating the
   *  image — the colour's luminance is 0.173, so it reaches only 4.46:1 against
   *  pure black and can never clear the 4.5 bar on any dark ground. `mono`
   *  solves it by discarding all four brand colours; `onhero` keeps them and
   *  whitens only the wordmark. Over `bg-dark-shade` use `ondark`: SOFTWARE
   *  clears 4.04:1 there, and the pink is the point.
   *
   *  In Figma the wordmark is one TEXT bound to `text/on dark/heading` — the
   *  same variable IRON already used — rather than a raw white. */
  variant?: 'default' | 'ondark' | 'mono' | 'onhero' | 'basic' | 'stack';
  /** kind="mark" only — swaps to the black knockout artwork. */
  mono?: boolean;
  /** kind="mark" / "element". The mark is drawn on an 8-unit grid in a 96-unit
   *  box, so the multiples of 24 keep the most edges on whole pixels.
   *
   *  MEASURED 2026-08-21, because the rule had been stated but never counted;
   *  re-measured and widened to the full ladder 2026-08-24.
   *  Share of path coordinates landing on a whole pixel, across all the artwork
   *  this component ships:
   *
   *              24px    48px    56px    64px    72px    96px   144px   192px
   *    mark     11.4%   18.9%    3.4%    5.7%   11.4%   18.9%   18.9%   19.0%
   *    element   2.1%    4.2%    0.5%    2.8%    2.1%   12.6%    4.2%   12.6%
   *
   *  THE LADDER IS EVERY MULTIPLE OF 24, and it is the same answer from two
   *  directions. Arithmetically: the artwork is drawn on 8-unit coordinates in a
   *  96-unit box, so a coordinate 8k lands whole at size S when kS/12 is an
   *  integer, which for every k means S divisible by 12 — and the second tier,
   *  the coordinates on 4-unit steps, needs 24. The table agrees: 24/48/72/96/
   *  144/192 measure 11.4-19.0% and NOTHING else clears 6.7%. Conventionally:
   *  the near-universal icon ladder is the 8-point grid (Material's baseline
   *  icon is 24 and its largest standard is 48; Tailwind's `size-*` runs
   *  16/20/24/32/40/48/64/80/96). Multiples of 24 are exactly where the industry
   *  ladder and this artwork's own arithmetic agree, so the union is that.
   *
   *  CONFIRMED AGAINST THE RENDER, not just the path data. Coordinates are a
   *  proxy; what a reader sees is pixels. Six marks were rasterised at nineteen
   *  sizes and the pixels that are neither transparent nor one of the flat
   *  colours the file declares — the anti-aliased ones — were counted, as a
   *  share of the mark's own painted area:
   *
   *      24px 29.7%  ·  36px 26.6%  ·  48px 18.3%  ·  60px 18.3%
   *      72px 14.5%  ·  96px 12.5%      — each one a local minimum
   *      40px 29.9%  ·  56px 25.7%  ·  64px 21.7%  — each beaten by a neighbour
   *
   *  Two corrections came out of that and both are in the numbers above. The
   *  share is only comparable BETWEEN NEIGHBOURS: a small mark has more edge per
   *  unit of area, so 24 reads worse than 96 for a reason that has nothing to do
   *  with the grid, and 36 actually renders cleaner than 24. And the local
   *  minima are every multiple of TWELVE, not only of 24 — 36 and 60 qualify.
   *  The union stays on the multiples of 24 because they are the strongest tier
   *  on both measures and the only ones also on the 8-point grid, but 36 and 60
   *  are legitimate if a frame ever needs them, and `check:logo-grid` will pass
   *  them.
   *
   *  WHAT THIS COSTS, and it is worth saying plainly: 16 and 32 are on the
   *  8-point grid and are NOT on this list, because one 96-unit drawing cannot
   *  be crisp at them — 5.7% each. A mark that must be sharp at 16 or 32 needs
   *  its own drawing at that size, which is what optical sizing is for and what
   *  Material and SF Symbols both ship. That is artwork work, not a prop.
   *
   *  56 AND 64 WERE REMOVED 2026-08-24. Both were added for one frame each — 56
   *  for the product mega-menu row (`FlyoutMenu-product`, Figma `1014:1921`,
   *  where the instance measures 56.000003814697266, i.e. dragged rather than
   *  chosen) and 64 for the iron-homepage changelog tiles (`2418:8806`). Neither
   *  is a local maximum: 56 draws 3.4% where 48 next door draws 18.9%, and 64
   *  draws 5.7% where 72 draws 11.4%. A union that grows by one frame at a time
   *  stops being a list of sizes that render well and becomes a list of sizes
   *  someone needed, which is what `height` already is for the non-square kinds.
   *
   *  THE ELEMENT ROW MOVED UNDER THIS COMMENT ONCE. Until 2026-08-24 it read a
   *  flat 0.3% at every size, with the conclusion that the grid rule is
   *  meaningless for the elements. That was TRUE when written (`1a6cff6`) and
   *  false three commits later: `91cd864` re-exported all ten off their pre-ramp
   *  colours and re-drew their paths. They are now on THIRDS rather than eighths,
   *  which is why their column peaks at 96/192 (12.6%) instead of tracking the
   *  marks. Two families, two grids — the ladder follows the MARK, where the
   *  difference is visible, and the elements are along for the ride until they
   *  are re-exported onto eighths.
   *
   *  32 WAS ASKED FOR ON 2026-08-31, by a consuming room that needed it for the
   *  product element — Figma `2407:12727` draws that element at 32 ten times.
   *  The paragraph above already answered it for the MARK (5.7%, and a mark that
   *  must be sharp at 32 needs its own drawing). What that paragraph did not
   *  say, because it treats the elements as along for the ride, is that 32 IS a
   *  local maximum for the ELEMENTS: 2.8% against 24's 2.1%. So the request was
   *  right about its own family and still cannot be granted — `size` is one
   *  union across both, a size has to be a local maximum in both, and for the
   *  marks 32 is beaten by 24 by exactly the 2x factor the rule uses. Adding it
   *  would turn `check:logo-grid` red.
   *
   *  Swept 8-96 with `node scripts/check-logo-grid.mjs --probe`, the two
   *  families agree everywhere except FOUR sizes, and they are worth naming
   *  because the next request will be one of them:
   *
   *     32   element only   64   element only
   *     36   mark only      60   mark only
   *
   *  Both families are local maxima at 8, 12, 24, 48, 72 and 96 — the multiples
   *  of 12 — and the union is that agreement from 24 up. Which is also why 64
   *  keeps coming back: like 32 it is a real step on the element ladder and a
   *  trough on the mark's. The way to serve either is artwork or a split prop,
   *  not a wider union.
   *
   *  `check:logo-grid` derives all of this from the SVGs on every run: the table
   *  above is compared to a fresh count, and every member of the union has to be
   *  a local maximum. Re-export the artwork and the gate re-derives the ladder
   *  rather than believing this paragraph.
   *
   *  Off-grid edges are visible on a hairline and these marks have none — they
   *  are solid colour blocks. Still: reach for a ladder size anywhere one will
   *  do, because the mark is where the difference is real. */
  size?: 24 | 48 | 72 | 96 | 144 | 192;
  /** kind="wordmark" / "lockup" — rendered height in px; width follows the aspect ratio. */
  height?: number;
  /** Wraps the logo in an <a>. */
  href?: string;
  /** Overrides the generated alt text. Pass "" for decorative use next to a visible name. */
  label?: string;
  basePath?: string;
  class?: string;
}

const {
  kind = 'mark',
  product = 'iron',
  brand = 'iron',
  variant = 'default',
  mono = false,
  size = 48,
  height = 32,
  href,
  label,
  basePath = 'assets',
  class: className,
} = Astro.props;

/* Product elements kept their original export filenames — the numbering is a Figma
   export artefact and several docs pages already link them, so they are not renamed. */
const ELEMENT_FILES: Record<ElementName, string> = {
  pdf: 'logo-01-pdf', word: 'logo-02-word', xl: 'logo-03-xl', ppt: 'logo-04-ppt',
  ocr: 'logo-05-ocr', barcode: 'logo-06-bc', qr: 'logo-07-qr', zip: 'logo-08-zip',
  print: 'logo-09-prt', webscraper: 'logo-010-ws', drawing: 'logo-11-drawing',
  securedoc: 'logo-12-securedoc', freetools: 'logo-13-freetools',
};

const PRODUCT_NAMES: Record<Product, string> = {
  pdf: 'IronPDF', wd: 'IronWord', xl: 'IronXL', ppt: 'IronPPT', qr: 'IronQR',
  ocr: 'IronOCR', bc: 'IronBarcode', prt: 'IronPrint', zip: 'IronZip',
  ws: 'IronWebScraper', suite: 'Iron Suite', iron: 'Iron Software',
};

let file: string;
let alt: string;

if (kind === 'lockup') {
  /* Figma's `Product logo` set — the mark and wordmark locked together. Sized by
     height like a wordmark, since neither layout is square: basic is 494x96,
     stack 173x203. */
  const v = variant === 'stack' ? 'stack' : 'basic';
  file = `lockup-${v}`;
  alt = 'Iron Software';
} else if (kind === 'wordmark') {
  /* suite has neither of the iron-only variants in Figma, so each falls back to
     the nearest artwork that exists rather than 404ing.

     They fall back DIFFERENTLY on purpose. `onhero` is an on-dark variant, so
     `ondark` is the nearest thing suite has; `default` would put dark text on a
     dark ground. `mono` falling back to `default` predates this and has the same
     smell — it is left alone rather than changed in passing, but it is worth a
     look. */
  const v = brand === 'suite' && variant === 'onhero' ? 'ondark'
          : brand === 'suite' && variant === 'mono' ? 'default'
          : variant;
  file = `wordmark-${brand}-${v}`;
  alt = brand === 'suite' ? 'Iron Suite' : 'Iron Software';
} else if (kind === 'element') {
  const key = product as ElementName;
  file = ELEMENT_FILES[key] ?? ELEMENT_FILES.pdf;
  alt = `${key.charAt(0).toUpperCase()}${key.slice(1)}`;
} else {
  const key = product as Product;
  file = `mark-${key}${mono ? '-mono' : ''}`;
  alt = PRODUCT_NAMES[key] ?? PRODUCT_NAMES.iron;
}

const src = `${basePath}/${file}.svg`;
const altText = label ?? alt;
const sizedByHeight = kind === 'wordmark' || kind === 'lockup';
const box = sizedByHeight ? { height: `${height}px` } : { width: `${size}px`, height: `${size}px` };
const Tag = href ? 'a' : 'span';
---

<Tag
  class:list={[
    'inline-flex items-center justify-center shrink-0 leading-[0]',
    /* Only the anchor form is interactive. The <style> version expressed this as
       `a.logo`; with utilities the condition has to be evaluated here, because a
       class list cannot ask what element it is on. */
    href && 'rounded-sm transition-opacity duration-[var(--duration-fast)] ease-default ' +
      'hover:opacity-85 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-border-selected focus-visible:outline-offset-[3px]',
    className,
  ]}
  style={box}
  href={href}
  aria-label={href && altText ? altText : undefined}
>
  {/* `.logo img` and the two size rules were descendant selectors; the utilities
      move onto the element they were always describing. */}
  <img
    class:list={['block max-w-full', sizedByHeight ? 'w-auto h-full' : 'size-full']}
    src={src}
    alt={altText}
    width={sizedByHeight ? undefined : size}
    height={sizedByHeight ? height : size}
  />
</Tag>
